# The bottom of a sum's walk. One row per reached layer that is not itself built from parts, with
# the route that reached it, so a layer at the bottom of two branches appears twice.
#
# Not the bottom of every walk
#   Which bottom applies depends on the quantity rather than on the graph. A sum over parts bottoms
#   out here, because below a layer built from nothing there is nothing left to add. A remainder
#   bottoms out earlier, at the first layer whose own nameplate and demand were not both scaled by
#   one factor that is a range, because subtracting them is legitimate there and the filed figure is
#   a claim its composer stands behind. composition/unsettled.sqlc is that condition, and
#   composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is that walk. A layer at the bottom meets it trivially,
#   which is why this relation looks like the general case and is not.
#
# A layer at the bottom is defined by what it is not
#   That is why this is an anti-join against a named relation rather than a `NOT EXISTS` inside a
#   condition. composition/fusions.sqlc is "the layers that are assembled"; a reached layer missing
#   from it is a layer somebody measured directly. Naming the positive relation makes the negation
#   readable and gives the same set a second use elsewhere.
#
# More than one row per (top, bottom) is double supply
#   Count the rows per (top layer, bottom layer), and anything above one is the same supply entering
#   one total twice. Both routes are in the `path` column, which is what makes the finding
#   something to act on instead of merely true.
#
# An anti-join that matches nothing looks like a working relation
#   It returns the whole left side, and nothing about the row count says the exclusion happened.
#   The law is worked out on every run rather than recorded: algebra/leaves.sqlc. The probe below
#   breaks it.
#
# `f.filing` is the right column to test
#   It is the join key, and `pm.part.composition` is NOT NULL, so a matched row can never carry a
#   NULL there. Testing a nullable column of the right-hand relation instead would keep matched rows
#   whose value happens to be NULL: an anti-join that quietly removes too little, with no symptom.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `leaves`. Rewrite this file as `EXCEPT` and algebra/leaves.sqlc must
#   fail, because the walk repeats rows on the key and `EXCEPT` removes the repeats.
-- asrt:Part followed to a layer that names no parts of its own.
SELECT d.*
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) d
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
       ON f.filing = d.filing AND f.layer = d.layer
WHERE f.filing IS NULL
